## What Is Encapsulation?

Encapsulation is the practice of controlling how an object's internal state is accessed and modified.

The important idea is not simply to make fields private. The deeper idea is that an object should control how its state can be observed or changed.

```dart
class BankAccount {
  double _balance = 0;

  double get balance => _balance;

  void deposit(double amount) {
    if (amount <= 0) {
      return;
    }

    _balance += amount;
  }
}
```

The class controls its internal state through a public operation instead of exposing unrestricted mutation.

---

## Dart Privacy

Dart does not use public, protected, and private keywords in the traditional way. An identifier beginning with `_` is private to the Dart library in which it is declared.

```dart
class User {
  String _email = '';

  String get email => _email;
}
```

The `_email` identifier is private to its library. This is an important Dart-specific distinction: `_` is library privacy, not simply class-level privacy.

---

## Why Encapsulation Matters

Suppose a product price must never be negative.

```dart
class Product {
  double price = 1000;
}
```

External code could do:

```dart
product.price = -500;
```

A better design controls the state:

```dart
class Product {
  double _price;

  Product(this._price) {
    assert(_price >= 0);
  }

  double get price => _price;

  set price(double value) {
    if (value < 0) {
      throw ArgumentError('Price cannot be negative.');
    }

    _price = value;
  }
}
```

The class can now maintain its invariant that price must not be negative.

---

## Getters

A getter provides read access through property syntax.

```dart
class User {
  final String firstName;
  final String lastName;

  User({
    required this.firstName,
    required this.lastName,
  });

  String get fullName => '$firstName $lastName';
}
```

A getter behaves like a property from the caller's perspective.

---

## Computed Properties

Getters are useful when a value can be derived from object state.

```dart
class ShoppingCart {
  final List<double> prices;

  ShoppingCart(this.prices);

  double get total {
    return prices.fold(
      0,
      (sum, price) => sum + price,
    );
  }
}
```

The total does not need to be stored separately. It can be calculated when requested.

---

## Getter vs Method

Use a getter when the result conceptually represents a property or state of the object:

```dart
cart.total
```

Use a method when the caller is asking the object to perform an operation:

```dart
cart.calculateShipping();
```

A useful mental model is:

```text
Property
↓
What is its value?

Method
↓
Perform this operation.
```

---

## Fields vs Explicit Getters

Dart intentionally makes fields and getters indistinguishable at the call site. This allows an implementation to evolve from a field to a getter without requiring changes to client code.

---

## Do Not Add Getters and Setters Unnecessarily

If a getter/setter pair adds no validation, computation, transformation, or access control, a direct field can be simpler.

---

## Setters

A setter controls how a property is assigned.

```dart
class Product {
  double _price = 0;

  double get price => _price;

  set price(double value) {
    if (value < 0) {
      throw ArgumentError('Price cannot be negative.');
    }

    _price = value;
  }
}
```

---

## Read-Only Properties

If a value should never change after construction, `final` is often the simplest design.

```dart
class User {
  final String id;

  User(this.id);
}
```

A private field plus public getter is more appropriate when the value can change internally but should not be directly assignable by external consumers.

---

## Protecting Mutable Collections

Returning a private mutable collection directly still exposes a mutation path.

```dart
List<String> get items => List.unmodifiable(_items);
```

The internal collection remains controlled by the class.

---

## Getters Are Functions

A getter is a special kind of function and can calculate a value dynamically.

---

## Getter Performance Consideration

A getter looks like a property but can execute computation. Avoid hiding expensive work, I/O, or significant side effects behind simple property syntax.

---

## Static Members

A static member belongs to the class rather than an individual object.

```dart
class MathConstants {
  static const double pi = 3.141592653589793;
}
```

---

## Instance vs Static Members

Instance members belong to an object and can access instance state. Static members belong to the class and do not operate on a particular instance.

---

## Static Methods Cannot Use `this`

A static method does not operate on a particular instance and therefore cannot access instance state through `this`.

---

## Static State

Static variables belong to the class rather than each object and can represent shared class-level state.

---

## Static Constants

Static constants can represent values conceptually associated with a class, although top-level constants can sometimes be clearer.

---

## Static Methods vs Top-Level Functions

If a function has no meaningful relationship to a type, a top-level function can be clearer than a class containing only static helpers.

---

## Static Is Not Singleton

A static member does not mean that a class has exactly one object. Singleton is a separate design pattern and will be studied separately.

---

## Encapsulation vs Abstraction

Encapsulation controls access to state and implementation.

Abstraction focuses on exposing the important contract while hiding unnecessary implementation complexity.

For example:

```dart
account.withdraw(500);
```

The caller does not need to manipulate internal balance state or validation rules.

Encapsulation protects the state.

Abstraction hides unnecessary implementation details.

This distinction is conceptual only at this stage; abstract classes and interfaces are covered later.

---

## Memory & Performance

Getters and setters are language features, but their implementations determine their cost.

```dart
double get total => price * quantity;
```

is inexpensive.

But:

```dart
List<Product> get products => List.from(_products);
```

creates a new list every time the getter is accessed.

Performance decisions should consider the actual work performed by getters, setters, static methods, and collection exposure.

